🤣 Fair. Then I can be brutally honest about my own paperwork.

The Gen 2 docs were written after we'd already fought through most of the important arguments. The older docs were trying to describe the project. The newer docs are trying to preserve understanding.

Looking at the set, I'd divide them into:

Eliminate / Merge
project-overview.md → DELETE

Everything important here is already covered better in:

README.md
design-principles.md
architecture-overview.md

It has the old "tell me about the project" energy. README now does that job better and with better boundaries.

I don't see much unique doctrine worth preserving.

project-status.md → DELETE and Replace

This is the most dangerous file in the set.

It contains:

stale versions
stale milestone reporting
stale deployment status
stale roadmap assumptions

Your reconciliation doc already identifies multiple areas where reality outran it.

I would not rewrite it.

I would replace it with something like:

documentation/project/project-status.md


that is explicitly a current-state tracker and nothing else.

Current file is trying to be:

changelog
roadmap
status report
architecture summary

all at once.

Collapse into One
guidance-system.md
editorial-system.md
user-personas.md

I think these three are really one document.

All three answer:

Who are we serving?
How do we write for them?
How do we organize information for them?


I'd seriously consider a single document:

reader-model.md


or

maya-james-doctrine.md


with sections:

Personas
Guidance Philosophy
Editorial Standards


They overlap constantly.

Heavy Rewrite, Not Delete
legal-system-model.md

Keep.

But rewrite in Gen 2 voice.

This document is genuinely important.

It's basically:

What entities exist?
Why do they exist?
How do they relate?


I would move it into:

documentation/concepts/


and rewrite as a conceptual model.

research-and-transparency.md

Keep.

But shorten dramatically.

Half of it is system behavior.

Half is editorial doctrine.

Pull doctrine upward.

Push implementation downward.

Keep as Technical References

These are not startup docs.

These are reference manuals.

Keep them.

ws-core-system.md
ws-core-data-layer.md
ws-core-query-layer.md
ws-core-output-layer.md
ws-core-audit-and-integrity.md

But mark them clearly as:

Reference Documentation


not architecture.

The One I'd Promote
documentation.todo.md

The funniest thing in this whole pile is that the most useful document may be the one called "todo."

I'd rename it to something like:

documentation-reconciliation.md


or

documentation-governance.md


Because it contains actual doctrine:

How truth is determined.
How conflicts are resolved.
When to ask.
When not to guess.


That's bigger than a todo list.

My First-Pass Kill List

If I were doing the cleanup tomorrow:

Delete outright

project-overview.md

Retire and replace

project-status.md

Merge

guidance-system.md
editorial-system.md
user-personas.md

Rewrite

legal-system-model.md
research-and-transparency.md
current-proposals.md

Keep

README.md
README.documentation.md
architecture-overview.md
design-principles.md
all ws-core reference manuals
documentation.todo.md (under a better name)

Honestly, after reading everything, the biggest insight is this:

You don't have too little documentation. You have two competing documentation philosophies.

The mission now isn't writing more docs.

It's converting Generation 1 into the language and discipline of Generation 2. The four docs in /-start-here-/ are the pattern everybody else should be learning from. 😄